y: flash-attn and xFormers register their own operators and autograd; SageAttention has no backward and is never used under autograd.","locale":"en"},"11386":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-167","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"**The chain.** A named backend comes first, then `fast`'s chain for the call, then the site's reference:","locale":"en"},"11387":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-168","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"Call","locale":"en"},"11388":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-169","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"`fast`'s chain","locale":"en"},"11389":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-170","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"CUDA, fp16/bf16, keys padded (`mask_info` with `keys` padded on one side: a preallocated cache, a padded batch)","locale":"en"},"11390":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-171","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"`flash`, `sdpa`","locale":"en"},"11391":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-172","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"CUDA, fp16/bf16, otherwise","locale":"en"},"11392":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-173","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"`sdpa`","locale":"en"},"11393":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-174","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"ROCm, fp16/bf16","locale":"en"},"11394":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-175","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"`flash`, `xformers`, `sdpa`","locale":"en"},"11395":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-176","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"fp32, or XPU, MPS, CPU","locale":"en"},"11396":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-177","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"`sdpa`","locale":"en"},"11397":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-178","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"**Resolution.** Each call is described by a `Call` (device kind, capability, dtype, head dims, GQA, one query or many, equal lengths, more than 1024 queries, mask kind, `mask_info`'s structure, a custom scale, causal, dropout, whether a gradient is needed, whether a CUDA graph is capturing), hashable, so a decode loop resolves once per `(site, Call)`. A backend is skipped when:","locale":"en"},"11398":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-179","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"its probe says it cannot run on the device (not installed, wrong version, no kernels, self-test failed);","locale":"en"},"11399":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-180","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"the call needs a backward pass or dropout it lacks;","locale":"en"},"11400":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-181","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"the site refuses quantised attention (`approx_ok=False`: every autoregressive model's, the Qwen decoder's, IndexTTS's GPT, VoxCPM's MiniCPM, GPT-SoVITS's T2S, Fish Speech's three, Whisper's) and the backend is approximate (SageAttention 2 and 3);","locale":"en"},"11401":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-182","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"its constraints refuse the call (device, capability, dtype, head dim, mask, causal over unequal lengths, scale);","locale":"en"},"11402":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-183","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"a CUDA graph is capturing and the backend is not `graph_safe`. Captures include attention (VoxCPM's local diffusion step, Fish Speech's fast transformer, Qwen3-TTS's code predictor); `graph_safe` is set only where a capture was replayed over new inputs and equalled the eager call on them (RTX 3060, torch 2.8): `sdpa`, `math`, `sdpa_flash`, `sdpa_efficient`, `sdpa_cudnn`, `flash` 2.8.3 and `xformers` 0.0.32. SageAttention 1.0.6 captured too, but the same backend runs 2.x, unchecked, and `sage3` was not tried. A backend that raises inside a capture is not marked broken: the capture fails, the step runs eagerly, and the backend is tried there.","locale":"en"},"11403":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-184","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"**Determinism in training.** A backend's backward pass either repeats bit for bit (`deterministic`), repeats when torch's deterministic algorithms are on (`deterministic_on_request`: FlashAttention, which is then called with `deterministic=True`; its forward pass is the same either way; on an RTX 3060 its gradients differed in each of 5 runs without and in none with), or does not (`sdpa`'s fused kernels, xFormers, `sdpa_cudnn`). A stage's attention report counts the calls with a gradient that a non-repeating backend computed (`nondeterministic`), and `train_perf.py --deterministic` records them per stage. Under `training.deterministic` with `training.attention = auto` the stage computes attention with `math`, which repeats. With a named backend, a call that needs a gradient on an accelerator (`Call.deterministic`: the mode is part of the call, so resolutions before and after it never mix) skips every backend whose backward does not repeat (`repeats`: the named one when it does not, then `sdpa`), and `math` closes the chain before the site's reference, usually SDPA itself. Calls without a gradient (a tokens stage, the frozen parts) keep the chain, and so does the CPU, whose kernels repeat. Before this, OmniVoice's float32 training attention under `flash` went to `sdpa` and two runs gave other weights; now it goes to `math`, and runs on two RTX 3060s give the same weights (the same as under a named `sdpa`; other than `auto`'s, whose tokens stage also computed with `math`).","locale":"en"},"11404":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-185","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#backends-and-the-chain","content":"A kernel that raises (anything but running out of memory or a cancel) is marked broken for that call, and the next backend takes it. Running out of memory propagates to the exact retry. Each fallback is logged once per (site, backend, reason). The request's `Run` counts what it used, and the output records it with a warning when anything fell back:","locale":"en"},"11405":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-186","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#structured-masks","content":"Fused kernels take masks as structure, not as a dense tensor (flash: `cache_seqlens`, `cache_leftpad`, `window_size`; xFormers: bottom-right causal and local masks). A site that builds its mask from a structure passes `mask_info=MaskInfo(keys, causal, window, padding)` beside the dense mask:","locale":"en"},"11406":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-187","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#structured-masks","content":"`keys`: `(batch, k_length)` booleans, the real keys; `padding`: `right` or `left` when each row is one run of real keys;","locale":"en"},"11407":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-188","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#structured-masks","content":"`causal`: aligned bottom-right (query *i* sees key *j* when `j ≤ i + n_k − n_q`); `window`: `j > i' − window`.","locale":"en"},"11408":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-189","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#structured-masks","content":"The reference ignores it and uses the dense mask, so `auto` is unchanged. Qwen's `mask_for` returns `Masked(dense, info)`; VoxCPM's steps, Seed-VC, GPT-SoVITS v4/v5 and Fish Speech's codec give theirs. With `dispatch.DEBUG` on (the tiny engines' tests), every call checks that `mask_info` describes its dense mask; `MIZUOTO_TEST_ACCEL=count` checks that every site of every tiny engine and trainer goes through the dispatcher.","locale":"en"},"11409":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-190","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#probes-and-the-doctor","content":"Each optional backend imports its package the first time a policy asks for it on a device, then checks it:","locale":"en"},"11410":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-191","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#probes-and-the-doctor","content":"the version: flash-attn ≥ 2.6; xFormers outside 0.0.21–0.0.26 (broken for large batches) and with its C++ kernels (`_has_cpp_library`: a wheel for another torch imports without them);","locale":"en"},"11411":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-192","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#probes-and-the-doctor","content":"the device and compute capability;","locale":"en"},"11412":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-193","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#probes-and-the-doctor","content":"a **self-test**: a 1 × 2 × 64 × D attention in fp16 against SDPA's math kernel in fp32, finite and within 2e-3 (2e-2 for SageAttention), which catches a wheel built for another torch or GPU.","locale":"en"},"11413":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-194","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#probes-and-the-doctor","content":"Results are cached per process and device. `mizuoto doctor` has an `acceleration` finding: the settings, each package (read from its metadata, without importing it), whether it runs on each GPU (`python -m mizuoto.runtime.accel` in a child process, when a GPU is there), and an install command that keeps torch (`--no-deps`; xFormers pinned to the release built for the installed torch).","locale":"en"},"11414":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-195","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#probes-and-the-doctor","content":"The registry, policies, fallbacks, strict mode and binding are tested on the CPU, with real models through every site (identical audio, metrics and weights with `auto`) and the optional backends' glue against fake packages computing the same maths. On NVIDIA (RTX 3090, torch 2.8, flash-attn 2.8.3, xFormers 0.0.32.post2, SageAttention 1.0.6) every backend was checked on every site's recorded calls, through every engine and every trainer: the results and what they decided. SageAttention 3 (Blackwell) is not checked.","locale":"en"},"11415":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-196","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"Process-wide GPU switches after ComfyUI's `--fast`, set once by the host when its runtime starts and by each training stage before its trainer loads (`features.apply`); outputs list them (`params.fast`). Each changes results; a change applies at the next start.","locale":"en"},"11416":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-197","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"Feature","locale":"en"},"11417":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-198","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"Effect","locale":"en"},"11418":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-199","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"`tf32`","locale":"en"},"11419":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-200","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"TensorFloat-32 matrix products and cuDNN convolutions (NVIDIA, Ampere on)","locale":"en"},"11420":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-201","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"`fp16_accumulation`","locale":"en"},"11421":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-202","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"fp16 products sum in fp16 (torch ≥ 2.7)","locale":"en"},"11422":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-203","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"`cudnn_benchmark`","locale":"en"},"11423":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-204","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"cuDNN tries kernels per shape","locale":"en"},"11424":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-205","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"`sdpa_reduced_precision`","locale":"en"},"11425":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-206","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#computefast","content":"SDPA's math kernel sums fp16/bf16 in their own precision (torch ≥ 2.5)","locale":"en"},"11426":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-207","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"`mizuoto.runtime.compile.Compiled` wraps one hot module in `torch.compile(dynamic=True)`: no shape buckets, so a new length does not recompile. Engines call `compiled or module`, so with the setting off the code path is the eager one. The setting is read at each load (`RuntimeOptions.compile`) and reaches models loaded after a change.","locale":"en"},"11427":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-208","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"Engine","locale":"en"},"11428":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-209","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"Compiled module","locale":"en"},"11429":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-210","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"F5-TTS","locale":"en"},"11430":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-211","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"the DiT, or E2's UNetT","locale":"en"},"11431":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-212","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"CosyVoice","locale":"en"},"11432":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-213","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"the flow estimator","locale":"en"},"11433":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-214","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"GPT-SoVITS v4, v5 Turbo","locale":"en"},"11434":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-215","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"the CFM estimator (each SoVITS model loaded, a voice's own too)","locale":"en"},"11435":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-216","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"IndexTTS 2, 2.5","locale":"en"},"11436":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-217","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"the s2mel DiT and BigVGAN (also turned on by its load option `use_torch_compile`)","locale":"en"},"11437":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-218","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"VoxCPM","locale":"en"},"11438":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-219","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"the local DiT","locale":"en"},"11439":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-220","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"Fish Speech S2 Pro","locale":"en"},"11440":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-221","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"its decoding steps' small operations, inside its CUDA graphs (below)","locale":"en"},"11441":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-222","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"`Compiled` is a plain attribute, not a submodule: state dicts and handles are unchanged.","locale":"en"},"11442":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-223","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"Any error from compiling or running the compiled code (no C++ compiler, an unsupported op) logs one warning and runs eagerly from then on; running out of memory still raises. It runs eagerly on MPS (`EAGER_DEVICES`) and above a streamed unit.","locale":"en"},"11443":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-224","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"It traces with its policy's `trace_run` (a compiled graph cannot read a context variable), one compiled call at a time while a policy other than `auto` is active.","locale":"en"},"11444":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-225","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"wenet's `add_optional_chunk_mask` takes a branch-free form under `torch.compiler.is_compiling()`: its `.item()` broke CosyVoice's graph.","locale":"en"},"11445":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-226","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"Inductor caches its code in `/tmp/torchinductor_<user>`. The Docker image has no C compiler, so it runs eagerly there.","locale":"en"},"11446":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-227","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"**Not exact.** Against eager on the CPU in fp32, the audio differs by at most 9e-5 for F5-TTS, 6e-5 for IndexTTS and 3e-5 for GPT-SoVITS. CosyVoice's mel stays within 5.7e-6, but HiFT's F0-driven source amplifies that to about 1e-2 in the audio (eager with another thread count does the same). For VoxCPM, float differences can move where the speech ends. The first request after a load pays the compilation (30 to 160 s on the CPU); the gains are in Performance record.","locale":"en"},"11447":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-228","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"**Fish Speech S2 Pro** compiles pieces of its decoding step instead of one module (`mizuoto_fishspeech/fused.py`):","locale":"en"},"11448":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-229","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"**The pieces:**","locale":"en"},"11449":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-230","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"each layer's halves around its attention, the slow transformer's 36 sharing one compiled graph per half and the fast transformer's 4 another;","locale":"en"},"11450":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-231","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"the attention over the cache;","locale":"en"},"11451":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-232","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"the step's embeddings and heads;","locale":"en"},"11452":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-233","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"the draws' probabilities.","locale":"en"},"11453":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-234","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"**With graphs:** the CUDA graphs capture the compiled kernels, so a frame is about 1,950 kernels instead of 5,900.","locale":"en"},"11454":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-235","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"**Randomness:** the draws are unchanged (the same uniforms from the same generator, in the same order); only the probabilities they are compared with differ, so the frames soon go elsewhere.","locale":"en"},"11455":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-236","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"**The slow attention:** the key length grows each step, so only that dimension is marked dynamic. With `dynamic=True` the head size was symbolic too, and inductor computed the attention's scale per element in float64: the attention ran twice as slow as eager on an RTX 3060, twice as fast with only the length dynamic.","locale":"en"},"11456":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-237","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"**When it runs:** only while the model is wholly on its device.","locale":"en"},"11457":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-238","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"**Output:** `applied[\"compile\"] = \"decoder\"`.","locale":"en"},"11458":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-239","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#torchcompile-computecompile","content":"**Measured:** ×1.15 per frame on an RTX 3060 (Performance record).","locale":"en"},"11459":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-240","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"An autoregressive model on a GPU launches thousands of small kernels per step (Qwen3-TTS: about 6 000 per 80 ms frame), and every wait for the device drains the queue, so the CPU's launch time and the GPU's work add up instead of overlapping. Each decoder now waits for the device once per frame or token, and with `compute.cuda_graphs` (on by default; NVIDIA only, ROCm's HIP graphs unchecked) its step's kernels are captured once and replayed at once. A graph replays exactly the kernels the eager code launched, with the same arguments, so its results are the eager code's bit for bit.","locale":"en"},"11460":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-241","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/graphs.py`:","locale":"en"},"11461":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-242","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"**`Graphs`**: the graphs of one model on one device, with their own stream and generator.","locale":"en"},"11462":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-243","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"`capture(fn, state, pool)` runs `fn` once first (lazy initialisation), puts back the tensors it changes (`state`) and the generator, then captures it. It calls `capture_begin`/`capture_end` itself: `torch.cuda.graph`'s `gc.collect()` and `empty_cache()` cost tens of milliseconds per capture, and a 24-layer step is 25 captures.","locale":"en"},"11463":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-244","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"The graphs' own generator is registered with each capture (`register_generator_state`) only before torch 2.14. From 2.14 a capture registers every generator it draws from by itself, and the call is a deprecated no-op that warned at each capture.","locale":"en"},"11464":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-245","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"After a capture the current stream waits for the capture stream: beginning a capture resets the generator's seed and offset tensors on the capture stream, and a first replay that overtook those kernels would draw from offset 0.","locale":"en"},"11465":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-246","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"A failed capture swaps in a fresh generator in the same state (an aborted capture leaves the old one unusable), and the caller runs eagerly; it is logged once.","locale":"en"},"11466":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-247","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"A failed capture can also leave its error pending in the CUDA runtime after the capture has ended (torch 2.15, CUDA 13.2), and the check after the thread's next kernel launch reports it once (allocating, copying, reading back and synchronizing do not, nor did a `zero_()`): `take_pending_error` runs a little eager work (an allocation, a copy from the host, a kernel, a read back) until it runs, dropping that report, so the eager fallback starts clean (`scripts/graph_failure_probe.py` shows which cleanup a torch release needs).","locale":"en"},"11467":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-248","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"Every capture also registers the device's default generator and marks it capturing; ending a failed capture does not unmark it, and from then on every draw from it outside a capture raised (\"Offset increment outside graph capture encountered unexpectedly\": `torch.randn` without a generator, dropout, inductor's benchmarks), in every engine, until the process ended. `release_default_generator` captures one kernel that draws nothing, which begins and ends its capture again: its seed and offset are unchanged (`tests/runtime/test_graphs.py`).","locale":"en"},"11468":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-249","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"`drawing(generator)` loads the request's generator into the graphs' own and writes it back after, so seeded requests draw the same numbers as eagerly.","locale":"en"},"11469":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-250","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"**`LayerSteps`**: a layer stack's one-token step as one graph per stretch between two attention calls, given each layer's `before`, `attend` and `after`. Attention runs eagerly between the graphs, over keys whose length grows each step, so the cache and the attention kernel are the eager code's; norms, projections, rotary embeddings and MLPs are replayed.","locale":"en"},"11470":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-251","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"**`DecoderSteps`**: `LayerSteps` for a `QwenDecoder`.","locale":"en"},"11471":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-252","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"**`signature(modules)`**: where the weights are. Graphs hold their addresses, so they are for a model wholly on its device and are dropped when its weights move. The exception is a `sequence` handle whose streamed units rest in page-locked memory (`ModelHandle.graphable`): the graphs copy those weights themselves each step (see Memory). Only Qwen3-TTS uses it; the other decoders streaming under a cap were already bound by the copies (about 12.4 GB/s) and gained nothing, VoxCPM 1.5 lost 2.2×, so they decode eagerly while streamed.","locale":"en"},"11472":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-253","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"Engine","locale":"en"},"11473":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-254","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"Waits per step, before → after","locale":"en"},"11474":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-255","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"Captured","locale":"en"},"11475":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-256","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"Qwen3-TTS (`qwen3tts/decode.py`)","locale":"en"},"11476":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-257","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"\\~35 a frame → 1","locale":"en"},"11477":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-258","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"sampling and the code predictor's 15 steps one graph each; the talker's step per stretch between attention calls (`DecoderSteps`)","locale":"en"},"11478":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-259","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"Fish Speech (`mizuoto_fishspeech/generate.py`)","locale":"en"},"11479":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-260","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"\\~22 a frame → 1","locale":"en"},"11480":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-261","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"the slow step around its attention (`LayerSteps`); the draws and the fast transformer one graph; kept per loaded model","locale":"en"},"11481":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-262","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"Fish Speech Drama 3 (`mizuoto_fishspeech/drama3/steps.py`)","locale":"en"},"11482":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-263","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"1 a frame (eager already)","locale":"en"},"11483":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-264","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"the tower around its 13 layers' attention, then the backbone around its 10 softmax attention layers (`Stretches`); the tower's again per sampling settings; not when the experts stream","locale":"en"},"11484":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-265","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"CosyVoice 2/3 (`cosyvoice/llm.py`)","locale":"en"},"11485":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-266","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"50–80 a token → 1","locale":"en"},"11486":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-267","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"the one-token steps (`DecoderSteps`) and the draw, a graph per EOS mask; upstream's nucleus loop replayed on the device (`nucleus_device`)","locale":"en"},"11487":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-268","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"VoxCPM (`voxcpm/model.py`)","locale":"en"},"11488":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-269","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"3 a patch → 1","locale":"en"},"11489":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-270","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"a whole patch per attention span and setting (`PatchDecoder`); the static caches kept with the model","locale":"en"},"11490":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-271","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"GPT-SoVITS (`gptsovits/t2s.py`)","locale":"en"},"11491":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-272","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"2 a step → 1","locale":"en"},"11492":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-273","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"the T2S's one-token steps around each layer's attention, a set per number of rows; sampling eager","locale":"en"},"11493":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-274","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"IndexTTS (`mizuoto_indextts/gpt.py`)","locale":"en"},"11494":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-275","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"\\~10 a beam step → 1","locale":"en"},"11495":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-276","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"the GPT-2's one-token steps around each layer's attention; the cache, its beam reorder and the sampling eager","locale":"en"},"11496":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-277","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cuda-graphs-computecuda_graphs","content":"The waits were removed with exact equivalents of upstream's idioms (masked assignments, list indices, Python scalars stored into tensors, `.item()` checks), and the stop flag is read once per step. `dispatch.DEBUG`'s mask check is skipped while a stream captures (it reads back; the warm-up checked).","locale":"en"},"11497":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-278","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#sync-free-sampling","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/sampling.py` reproduces transformers' logits processors in their order (repetition penalty, min new tokens, suppression, temperature, top-k, top-p) with nothing that waits for the device:","locale":"en"},"11498":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-279","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#sync-free-sampling","content":"**`multinomial1(probs, generator)`** is `torch.multinomial`'s own one-sample computation, `argmax(p / Exp(1))`, without its two validity checks (each a wait). The same ids and generator state on the CPU and CUDA; elsewhere it calls `torch.multinomial`. NaN probabilities are flagged in a tensor (`invalid`) the caller reads at its one wait, and raise `torch.multinomial`'s error then.","locale":"en"},"11499":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-280","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#sync-free-sampling","content":"**`multinomial_draws(probs, n, generator)`**: the draw of several without replacement, `topk(p / Exp(1), n)` (IndexTTS's beams).","locale":"en"},"11500":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-281","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#sync-free-sampling","content":"Suppression and EOS masks are built once per device; `repetition_penalty_seen` applies the penalty over a mask of the drawn ids, so shapes do not grow.","locale":"en"},"11501":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-282","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#sync-free-sampling","content":"The shared Qwen decoder, without autograd, norms and rotates q and k together on the CPU (on a GPU apart, as transformers does, unless the projections are fused: CUDA sizes a reduction by its tensor, so the joined norm differed in the last bit now and then) and uses `F.rms_norm` where a self-test on the device shows the same bits as the float32-upcast norm (`_fused_norm_exact`: one kernel instead of eight on CUDA in bf16/fp16 with recent torch). `mask_for(padded=…)` skips its device check when the caller knows there is no padding.","locale":"en"},"11502":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-283","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#sync-free-sampling","content":"**Fused projections** (`engines.qwen3tts.load_options.fused_projections`, off) stack each layer's q/k/v and gate/up weights into one linear layer each at load (`QwenDecoder.fuse`; real `ops.Linear`s, so they still stream). Inference only. cuBLAS picks other kernels for the wider matrices, so the bits differ on CUDA; outputs list `fused_projections`. CosyVoice 2 and 3 offer the same for their Qwen2 language model (`engines.cosyvoice.load_options.fused_projections`): 1 % faster per second of audio in fp32 on an RTX 3060, where the audio stayed identical, 2–5 % with `fp16`, where it changes. OmniVoice's decoder was fused too and gained nothing (it runs over the whole sequence every step: large products the fusion does not speed up), so it is not offered (Performance).","locale":"en"},"11503":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-284","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"`mizuoto/core/compute/cpu.py` (standard library only) is applied by the host before torch is imported (`cpu.apply`, when the runtime is built) and handed to the processes it starts (`child_env`: training stages, job workers). Neither changes a result. Variables the environment sets itself win; off glibc and off Linux nothing changes.","locale":"en"},"11504":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-285","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"Setting","locale":"en"},"11505":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-286","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"`auto` on Linux","locale":"en"},"11506":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-287","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"What it does","locale":"en"},"11507":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-288","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"`compute.cpu_allocator`","locale":"en"},"11508":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-289","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"`tuned`","locale":"en"},"11509":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-290","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"glibc's `mmap` threshold fixed at 32 MB and up to 512 MB of free heap kept for reuse (`mallopt` here, `GLIBC_TUNABLES` for children); torch's large tensors on transparent huge pages (`THP_MEM_ALLOC_ENABLE`), except in training stages under `training.memory = lowest`, where AdamW stepping in RAM allocates gigabytes every step and each huge page stalls in the kernel's compaction (2.2× slower). A forward allocates and frees tensors of a few to tens of megabytes per layer; with glibc's defaults each is mapped fresh and its pages zeroed. `system` keeps the defaults","locale":"en"},"11510":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-291","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"`compute.cpu_threads`","locale":"en"},"11511":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-292","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"`cores`","locale":"en"},"11512":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-293","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"OpenMP threads bound to distinct physical cores (`OMP_PLACES=cores`, `OMP_PROC_BIND=close`). Left to the scheduler two threads sometimes share a core, and that process runs a third slower for its whole life. `off` leaves placement to the scheduler (better when other busy programs share the cores)","locale":"en"},"11513":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-294","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#cpu-settings","content":"**Huge pages under memory pressure** (measured 2026-11-05; Qwen3-TTS 1.7B in fp32, the audio and the weights identical every time): on a server's i9-9900K (8 cores, 31 GB, `defrag=madvise`, the page cache filled before each run, under 400 MB free) huge pages made a long case 6–8 % slower (143.5–149.7 s against 135.6–138.0 s warm) and loading 2–5 s slower, with 188,000–297,000 direct-compaction stalls a round against none; on a Ryzen 9 5900X with 125 GB (98 GB of it page cache) the CPU fine-tune's steps were 8 % faster with them (7.4–7.5 s against 8.0–8.2 s), and its first step (AdamW's state, 13.6 GB of zeros) at most 2 s slower. The 12.6 s once measured for that state was the same compaction, not the zeroing. `auto` keeps huge pages; the user page says when to turn them off.","locale":"en"},"11514":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-295","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#for-engine-authors","content":"Route every attention through `accel.attention` with a `Site` per call site; give a hand-written upstream attention as the site's `reference`; pass `mask_info` where the mask is built from a structure.","locale":"en"},"11515":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-296","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#for-engine-authors","content":"Draw with `multinomial1` / `multinomial_draws` and keep decoding steps free of waits (no `.item()`, `tolist()`, Python scalars into tensors, masked assignment) except one per step.","locale":"en"},"11516":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-297","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#for-engine-authors","content":"For CUDA graphs: run the step over fixed tensors, capture with `Graphs` / `LayerSteps` only when `graphs.usable(device, runtime.options.cuda_graphs)` and the model is whole on its device (`handle.loaded`; `handle.graphable()` also takes a streamed `sequence` handle, measured faster only for Qwen3-TTS), keep the graphs with the loaded model and drop them when `signature` changes; draw inside `drawing(ctx.generator_for(device))`. Capture and replay under `torch.cuda.device(graphs.device)`: a replay runs on the current device's stream, so a model on `cuda:1` replayed while `cuda:0` is current faults with an illegal memory access (CosyVoice did until 2026-10-18).","locale":"en"},"11517":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-298","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#for-engine-authors","content":"Wrap a heavy, repeatedly called module with `compile_module(module, runtime.options.compile, name)` and call `compiled or module`.","locale":"en"},"11518":{"id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration-299","page_id":"/en/docs/development-acceleration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-acceleration#for-engine-authors","content":"Check: the tiny engine's audio is identical with graphs on and off on CUDA, with a capture made to fail, and with `MIZUOTO_TEST_ACCEL=count`. See Adding an engine and Testing.","locale":"en"},"11519":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"page","content":"HTTP API and command line","breadcrumbs":["Mizuoto Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api","locale":"en"},"11520":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api","content":"How the FastAPI app is assembled, how the security middleware and errors map to HTTP, how events enter the OpenAPI schema and become the web UI's types, and how to add a route and a command.","locale":"en"},"11521":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"The app: `create_app()`","locale":"en"},"11522":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#dependencies-depspy","content":"Dependencies: `deps.py`","locale":"en"},"11523":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#security-middleware-securitypy","content":"Security middleware: `security.py`","locale":"en"},"11524":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"Errors → HTTP: `errors.py`","locale":"en"},"11525":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#writing-a-route","content":"Writing a route","locale":"en"},"11526":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-runtime-routes","content":"The runtime routes","locale":"en"},"11527":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#openapi-with-events","content":"OpenAPI with events","locale":"en"},"11528":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#types-for-the-web-ui","content":"Types for the web UI","locale":"en"},"11529":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#adding-a-route","content":"Adding a route","locale":"en"},"11530":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-command-line","content":"The command line","locale":"en"},"11531":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-api#adding-a-command","content":"Adding a command","locale":"en"},"11532":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api","content":"The API (`mizuoto/api/`) and the command line (`mizuoto/cli/`) are two thin shells over one core: parse the input, call one core method, shape the output. This page covers both shells and their conventions; the core itself is in Architecture, and how the web UI consumes these endpoints is in Web UI.","locale":"en"},"11533":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"`create_app(services, bound_host, bound_port, extra_hosts, serve_ui, api_prefix)` in `mizuoto/api/app.py` builds the whole application. The caller owns the services (`mizuoto webui` builds them with `build_services(start_background=True)`, `mizuoto.embed.MizuotoServer` inside a host application); the app only hangs them on `app.state.services`.","locale":"en"},"11534":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"Mount","locale":"en"},"11535":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"Contents","locale":"en"},"11536":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"`{prefix}/api/v1/...`","locale":"en"},"11537":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"One router module per resource, `routers/<resource>.py`, each with `APIRouter(prefix=\"/v1/<resource>\")`","locale":"en"},"11538":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"`{prefix}/v1/...`","locale":"en"},"11539":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"`routers/openai.py`: the OpenAI-compatible `POST /v1/audio/speech` and `GET /v1/models`","locale":"en"},"11540":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"`{prefix}/ws`","locale":"en"},"11541":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"`SocketBridge` in `sockets.py`: EventBus → socket.io, server to client only","locale":"en"},"11542":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"`{prefix}/docs`, `{prefix}/openapi.json`","locale":"en"},"11543":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"FastAPI's Swagger page and the schema","locale":"en"},"11544":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"`{prefix}/`","locale":"en"},"11545":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"`SPAStaticFiles` from `static.py`, registered last, so it only gets paths nothing else matched","locale":"en"},"11546":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"`api_prefix` moves all of the above under one sub-path, so a host application can mount it (see Embedding). The web UI needs no change: it derives its root from the URL of its own script (`webui/src/api/baseUrl.ts`).","locale":"en"},"11547":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#the-app-create_app","content":"Middleware, in order: an optional `CORSMiddleware` (only when `server.allowed_origins` is non-empty), `GZipMiddleware` (above 4 KB) and `SecurityMiddleware`. The lifespan only starts and stops the socket bridge.","locale":"en"},"11548":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#dependencies-depspy","content":"Services come from `app.state`, not from a global:","locale":"en"},"11549":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#dependencies-depspy","content":"`is_local(request)` means \"a browser on the server's own machine\": the client address is loopback **and** the requested host name is a loopback name. A tunnel or reverse proxy on the same machine also connects from loopback, but forwards its public `Host`, so it is not local. Routes that need it (browsing server folders, registering audio by server path, `/models/identify`, `/models/import`, `reveal`, `POST /voices/{id}/weights`, `/engines/{id}/ops/{op}`, a dataset's folder import) declare `dependencies=[LocalDep]` and fail with 403 `not_local`. Server paths must also lie within `paths.browse_roots`; that check is in the core.","locale":"en"},"11550":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#security-middleware-securitypy","content":"An API that can delete and move files checks three things on every HTTP and WebSocket request (before any route; plain ASGI):","locale":"en"},"11551":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#security-middleware-securitypy","content":"**Host**: when bound to loopback, `Host` must be `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `::1`, the bound host or a name in `extra_hosts`, else 400 `bad_host`. This blocks DNS rebinding. `extra_hosts` is kept by reference, so a tunnel's public name added later is accepted at once.","locale":"en"},"11552":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#security-middleware-securitypy","content":"**Origin**: a state-changing request (not `GET`/`HEAD`/`OPTIONS`) and every WebSocket handshake, when it carries `Origin`, must come from the page's own origin or one in `server.allowed_origins`; without `Origin`, `Sec-Fetch-Site` must be `same-origin`, `none` or absent. Else 403 `bad_origin`.","locale":"en"},"11553":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#security-middleware-securitypy","content":"**Token**: when `server.access_token` is set, requests under `/api/`, `/ws/`, `/v1/`, `/openapi.json` and `/docs` must carry it, as `Authorization: Bearer`, the `mizuoto_token` cookie or a `token` query parameter, compared with `hmac.compare_digest`. Missing or wrong is 401 `auth_required`. `/api/v1/app/health` is exempt: the UI shell must load before it can ask for a token.","locale":"en"},"11554":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#security-middleware-securitypy","content":"A refused WebSocket is closed with 1008. socket.io's `connect` handler (`SocketBridge._on_connect`) checks `auth.token` once more.","locale":"en"},"11555":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"Every core error (`mizuoto/core/errors.py`) carries `code`, `http_status` and `exit_code`; `install_error_handlers()` turns any `MizuotoError` into one shape:","locale":"en"},"11556":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"`ERROR_RESPONSES` declares an `ErrorResponse` model for 400/403/404/409 on every router, so the generated client knows the error shape. Part of the mapping:","locale":"en"},"11557":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"Exception","locale":"en"},"11558":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"`code`","locale":"en"},"11559":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"HTTP","locale":"en"},"11560":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"Exit code","locale":"en"},"11561":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"`NotFoundError`","locale":"en"},"11562":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"`not_found`","locale":"en"},"11563":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"404","locale":"en"},"11564":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"2","locale":"en"},"11565":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"`ConflictError` / `BusyError`","locale":"en"},"11566":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"`conflict` / `busy`","locale":"en"},"11567":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"409","locale":"en"},"11568":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"3","locale":"en"},"11569":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-api#errors--http-errorspy","content":"`ValidationError`, `InvalidPathError`","locale":"en"},"11570":{"id":"/en/docs/development-api-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-api","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/dev