ags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"VoxCPM v2-clone-en","locale":"en"},"12765":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"6.72","locale":"en"},"12766":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"76.91","locale":"en"},"12767":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"language model 75.6, vae 1.3","locale":"en"},"12768":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"VoxCPM v15-clone-en","locale":"en"},"12769":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"5.60","locale":"en"},"12770":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"21.82","locale":"en"},"12771":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"language model 20.7, vae 1.1","locale":"en"},"12772":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"**Matrix products dominate** (`F.linear`/`addmm`: 50–90 % of the time). The autoregressive LMs run one token at a time against fp32 weights, so they are bound by memory bandwidth (Qwen3-TTS's 1.7B talker reads about 6.8 GB a frame at about 36 GB/s). Faster kernels, lower precision or other thread counts would change the sums.","locale":"en"},"12773":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"**VoxCPM 2 spent 40 of its 77 s in attention**: each step attended over the whole 8192-position static cache under a mask, though a few hundred positions were filled.","locale":"en"},"12774":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"**Allocation**: every layer allocates and frees tensors of a few to tens of megabytes, and with glibc's defaults each is mapped fresh and its pages zeroed by the kernel.","locale":"en"},"12775":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"Change","locale":"en"},"12776":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"Why it is exact","locale":"en"},"12777":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"`compute.cpu_allocator` and `compute.cpu_threads` (Acceleration)","locale":"en"},"12778":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"where memory comes from and where threads run, not what is computed; the same thread count splits the work the same way","locale":"en"},"12779":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"VoxCPM attends over the cache up to the next multiple of 256 past the current length, mask kept, instead of all 8192 positions (on the CPU only, since G1: see GPU checks)","locale":"en"},"12780":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"masked positions add exact zeros, and the CPU's SDPA gives the same bits for any cut at a multiple of 64 (every length up to 3100, bf16 and fp32, batch 1 and 2)","locale":"en"},"12781":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"DiT rotary embedding: cos and sin once per forward, no multiplication by 1, no concatenation of an empty tail (F5-TTS, CosyVoice 3, GPT-SoVITS v4/v5)","locale":"en"},"12782":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"the same values","locale":"en"},"12783":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"CosyVoice's flow U-Net builds its per-head mask once per forward, not in each of