,"13166":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-504","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"5,103","locale":"en"},"13167":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-505","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"Peak MB is the request's peak allocation, every model loaded in the process included. GPT-SoVITS's streamed case decodes the whole text before its first chunk (first audio 0.95 s of 0.96 s); VoxCPM's streams by patch (0.14 s).","locale":"en"},"13168":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-506","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"**Repeats.** Every case gives the same audio run to run and in a new process, in the default mode, except VoxCPM 2's first request. `--deterministic` changes the bits of CosyVoice 2, IndexTTS 2, OmniVoice and VoxCPM (other cuBLAS and cuDNN algorithms), repeats as well, and lists no operation without a deterministic kernel. So later GPU rounds compare outputs exactly in the default mode, with VoxCPM's first request thrown away.","locale":"en"},"13169":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-507","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"**VoxCPM's first request.** Snake is a TorchScript function, as upstream's is. On CUDA, TorchScript's profiling executor runs a function's first call for a shape as separate operations (bit-identical to eager code and to the CPU), then runs a fused kernel that differs by up to 4.8·10⁻⁷. A shape it has not seen falls back to the unfused operations until it is profiled. So which calls are fused depends on what the process has already decoded, and a warm-up at load cannot make every request repeat. With the fuser off (an experiment; native code never sets it) the first request agrees with the later ones. The fuser also compiles its kernels on the default device: a process working on `cuda:1` opens a 256 MB context on `cuda:0` (seen in VoxCPM's and GPT-SoVITS's training, whose WaveNet gate is scripted too). The CPU is unaffected (no fusion there). (Since fixed: Snake runs eagerly, the same on every request and on the CPU; about 1 % slower in the VAE. See VoxCPM.)","locale":"en"},"13170":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-508","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"**Where the time goes** (one extra run under torch's profiler, per phase: the device's busy time against the phase's warm time without the profiler, which slows the host's side):","locale":"en"},"13171":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-509","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"Bound by the device's work (about 90 % busy or more): F5-TTS's DiT, IndexTTS's s2mel, BigVGAN and 1.5's GPT, CosyVoice 3's flow, Fish Speech's, Qwen3-TTS 1.7B's, VoxCPM's and OmniVoice's language models. (Capturing OmniVoice's step forward as a CUDA graph, tried in G2, gave 2 %.)","locale":"en"},"13172":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-510","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"Bound by launches: GPT-SoVITS's GPT (about 56 %) and CosyVoice 2's language model (about 69 %).","locale":"en"},"13173":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-511","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"Host waits: about one per generated token in every autoregressive decoder (GPT-SoVITS v2 157 for 157 tokens, Fish Speech 92 for about 87 frames, Qwen3-TTS 82 for about 73 frames), as designed.","locale":"en"},"13174":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-512","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"**Working memory** against each phase's estimate (the estimate must be at or above what the phase uses): language models use 2–17 % of their estimates (Qwen3-TTS's talker 28 MB of 328, Fish Speech's 128 of 733). Two decoders use more than estimated: IndexTTS 1.5's BigVGAN 326 MB against 279, VoxCPM 1.5's VAE 278 MB against 183; VoxCPM 2's VAE is close (190 against 203). The OOM retry covers these on a tight GPU; the estimates are to be raised.","locale":"en"},"13175":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-513","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"**GPU parity with 0.1**, where 0.1 defined GPU behaviour (each side run twice; both repeat themselves exactly):","locale":"en"},"13176":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-514","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"Case","locale":"en"},"13177":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-515","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"0.1 on the GPU","locale":"en"},"13178":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-516","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"Native against 0.1","locale":"en"},"13179":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-517","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-baseline-g0","content":"F5-TTS clone-en, fp16","locale":"en"},"13180":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-518","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[]