VoxCPM 2","locale":"en"},"14712":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-311","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#t4-results","content":"1.45 → 1.52 / 1.52 → 1.88 / 0.55 → 0.68 / 0.91 → 0.88","locale":"en"},"14713":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-312","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#t4-results","content":"0 / 0 (IndexTTS 1 / 1)","locale":"en"},"14714":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-313","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#t4-results","content":"within 0.004","locale":"en"},"14715":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-314","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#t4-results","content":"within 0.03","locale":"en"},"14716":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-315","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#t4-results","content":"pass (GPT-SoVITS's worst clip within noise)","locale":"en"},"14717":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-316","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#t4-results","content":"`compute.attention=fast` without flash-attn installed is SDPA everywhere, so its audio is identical. With it, F5-TTS has no padded half-precision call for FlashAttention. GPT-SoVITS v4's DiT gives one row of key lengths for its guided batch of two, which flash-attn refused (`seqlens_k must have shape (batch_size)`) until the backend expanded the lengths to the query's batch; it now takes those 704 calls.","locale":"en"},"14718":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-317","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#t4-results","content":"The first opt-in runs exposed a harness bug: requests and the parity cases had put engine options under `options`, which the request model does not have and pydantic ignores, so `packed_guidance` and six T3 cases (GPT-SoVITS's cut5, batch and serial cases, VoxCPM's continuation and retry) had run with the defaults. They now use `engine_options`; rerun on the CPU, the six are identical to 0.1 and upstream sample for sample with their options applied.","locale":"en"},"14719":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-318","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#web-ui-tests","content":"`mizuoto/webui/src/**/*.test.ts` run with vitest; `python scripts/dev.py test-web` runs `bun run test` and then `bun run typecheck` (vue-tsc). Besides unit tests of components and stores, a few rule tests guard the whole UI: `imports.test.ts`, `tokens.test.ts`, `layout.test.ts`, `ui/layers.test.ts` and `i18n/i18n.test.ts` (the same keys in both languages); see Web UI.","locale":"en"},"14720":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-319","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#web-ui-tests","content":"`python scripts/dev.py typegen-check` regenerates `schema.d.ts` and compares it with the committed one; it fails when the API changed and the types were not regenerated.","locale":"en"},"14721":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-320","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#web-ui-tests","content":"**Browser tests.** `tests/e2e/` (marker `e2e`) drives the built UI (`mizuoto/webui/dist`) in headless Chromium through Playwright, served by a real server (`python -m mizuoto webui`) on a new data folder with the dummy engine, so nothing is downloaded: every screen loads without console errors or uncaught exceptions, a speak runs from the text to its row in the results and in History, a 320 and a 390 px window neither scroll sideways nor lose a screen (the bottom bar's **More**), and the Chinese UI loads. Run them with `python scripts/dev.py web` (the build) and `python scripts/dev.py test-e2e`, after `pip install playwright` and `python -m playwright install chromium`; without them, or without a build, they skip. Selectors are roles, labels and the components' own classes (`nav.bar`, `section.results li.row`).","locale":"en"},"14722":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-321","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#web-ui-tests","content":"The docs site (`site/`) has its own vitest tests; `src/lib/i18n-content.test.ts` requires a `<slug>.en.mdx` for every Chinese `<slug>.mdx` and the same page order in both sidebars.","locale":"en"},"14723":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-322","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs on every push to a branch and every pull request, with torch's CPU build (`https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu`). Mizuoto and the two gated packages are released on their own, so each is checked on its own, and a change runs only the checks for what it changes. Packages are installed with uv (`astral-sh/setup-uv`, `UV_SYSTEM_PYTHON=1`: into the Python that `setup-python` provides, no virtual environment; the cache stays off, since unpacked torch wheels would crowd the 10 GB cache limit).","locale":"en"},"14724":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-323","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"The first job, `scope`, reads the changed files (a push's from the compare API, a pull request's from its file list) and `python scripts/ci.py scope` maps them to checks:","locale":"en"},"14725":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-324","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Changed","locale":"en"},"14726":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-325","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Checks","locale":"en"},"14727":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-326","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`packages/native/<id>/**`","locale":"en"},"14728":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-327","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"that package's `gated` and `oldest-mizuoto` jobs, and `torch` for it","locale":"en"},"14729":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-328","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`mizuoto/webui/**`","locale":"en"},"14730":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-329","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`web`, `browser`","locale":"en"},"14731":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-330","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`site/**`","locale":"en"},"14732":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-331","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`site` (the docs site's tests and build)","locale":"en"},"14733":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-332","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"anything else under `mizuoto/`, `tests/`, `scripts/`, `pyproject.toml`, or a new path","locale":"en"},"14734":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-333","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`host`, `web` (its API types come from the code), `browser`, `torch`, and both packages' jobs: they run on Mizuoto's runtime at this commit","locale":"en"},"14735":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-334","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`.github/workflows/**`, `scripts/ci.py`","locale":"en"},"14736":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-335","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"everything","locale":"en"},"14737":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-336","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`README*.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `LICENSE`, `Dockerfile`, `.gitignore`…","locale":"en"},"14738":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-337","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"nothing","locale":"en"},"14739":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-338","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"A new branch, a manual run, a list the compare API cuts short (300 files) or a failure to read it checks everything; `tests/test_ci.py` covers the mapping.","locale":"en"},"14740":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-339","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Job","locale":"en"},"14741":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-340","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Environment","locale":"en"},"14742":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-341","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Steps","locale":"en"},"14743":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-342","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`host`","locale":"en"},"14744":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-343","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Ubuntu, Windows, macOS × Python 3.10, 3.11, 3.12, 3.13, 3.14","locale":"en"},"14745":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-344","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`uv pip install torch` from the CPU index, then `-e . pytest pytest-cov ruff ty httpx tomli` (Mizuoto alone); in every cell `dev.py lint` (the whole repository), `dev.py typecheck-py --python-version <cell> --python-platform <cell> mizuoto tests scripts` (the standard library's stubs differ between Pythons and platforms) and `python -m pytest -q tests`; each of the three runs even when another failed","locale":"en"},"14746":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-345","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`gated`","locale":"en"},"14747":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-346","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"each package in scope × Ubuntu, Windows, macOS × Python 3.10, 3.14","locale":"en"},"14748":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-347","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`-e . -e packages/native/<id>`; `ruff check`, `ruff format --check` and `typecheck-py` of `packages/native/<id>` (sources and tests), `pytest` of its `tests/`","locale":"en"},"14749":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-348","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`oldest-mizuoto`","locale":"en"},"14750":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-349","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"each package in scope, Ubuntu, Python 3.12","locale":"en"},"14751":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-350","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"The package on the oldest Mizuoto its `pyproject.toml` allows (`python scripts/ci.py lower-bound <id>`), from PyPI: its tests run outside the checkout, with only `tests/` on `PYTHONPATH`, so `import mizuoto` finds the released one (the range check, which compares the range with Mizuoto at this commit, is left out)","locale":"en"},"14752":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-351","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`torch`","locale":"en"},"14753":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-352","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Ubuntu, Python 3.12, torch 2.8.0 and the latest release","locale":"en"},"14754":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-353","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"The tests in scope against the oldest torch Mizuoto supports and the newest, to catch torch's API drift","locale":"en"},"14755":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-354","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`web`","locale":"en"},"14756":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-355","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Ubuntu, Python 3.11, bun","locale":"en"},"14757":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-356","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`dev.py web-install`, `dev.py test-web`, `dev.py typegen-check`","locale":"en"},"14758":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-357","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`browser`","locale":"en"},"14759":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-358","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Ubuntu, Python 3.11, bun, Playwright's Chromium (`playwright install --with-deps chromium`)","locale":"en"},"14760":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-359","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`dev.py web-install`, `dev.py web` (the build), `dev.py test-e2e`","locale":"en"},"14761":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-360","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`site`","locale":"en"},"14762":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-361","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Ubuntu, bun","locale":"en"},"14763":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-362","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`bun install --frozen-lockfile`, `bun run check`, `bun run test`, `bun run build` in `site/`","locale":"en"},"14764":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-363","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`passed` (\"CI passed\")","locale":"en"},"14765":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-364","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Ubuntu","locale":"en"},"14766":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-365","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"Fails when a job above failed or was cancelled, passes when they passed or were skipped: the one check to require in branch protection","locale":"en"},"14767":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-366","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"A newer run of the same branch cancels the older one. A push to `main` that changes a version file starts the release workflow, which runs `ci.yml` as its checks for what the push changed; CI's own run of that push skips every job (`scope` sees the version file), so the checks run once. Tags start only the release workflow, whose checks are everything. Pushing the docs site to GitHub Pages is `site.yml`'s, not CI's (see Release).","locale":"en"},"14768":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-367","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"`python scripts/dev.py check` runs all of it locally (on your platform and Python only, with the gated packages installed, on Mizuoto at your checkout). The contract suite and the parity harness need models (and, for parity, the reference environments) weighing tens of gigabytes, so they stay out of CI; the release workflow has its own wheel smoke test (see Release).","locale":"en"},"14769":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-368","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#ci","content":"CI has only a CPU build of torch. The GPU paths (CUDA graphs, the optional attention backends, weight streaming's asynchronous prefetch) are tested there for their logic; how they behave on a GPU has to be checked on a machine with one, with the contract suite and the parity harness.","locale":"en"},"14770":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"page","content":"Trainers","breadcrumbs":["Mizuoto Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training","locale":"en"},"14771":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training","content":"How training methods are declared in a manifest, how datasets are added, prepared and checked, how a method's stages run one by one as child processes of Mizuoto's own interpreter, how fingerprints decide what is stale, how trainers are written, and what exports register.","locale":"en"},"14772":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Training methods in the manifest","locale":"en"},"14773":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#datasets","content":"Datasets","locale":"en"},"14774":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#experiments-fingerprints-and-stale-stages","content":"Experiments, fingerprints and stale stages","locale":"en"},"14775":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#the-train-job","content":"The train job","locale":"en"},"14776":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#why-a-child-process","content":"Why a child process","locale":"en"},"14777":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#trainer-and-stagecontext","content":"Trainer and StageContext","locale":"en"},"14778":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#memory","content":"Memory","locale":"en"},"14779":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#exports","content":"Exports","locale":"en"},"14780":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#omnivoices-trainer-as-an-example","content":"OmniVoice's trainer, as an example","locale":"en"},"14781":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-training#adding-a-trainer","content":"Adding a trainer","locale":"en"},"14782":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training","content":"Every trainable engine trains natively, in Mizuoto's own environment, on the same modules it runs inference with. Each trainer reproduces its upstream recipe: given the same seed on the CPU, its losses, learning rates and saved weights equal byte for byte those of the upstream scripts (as Mizuoto 0.1 ran them; for VoxCPM, upstream's own script). Training needs no environment and no extra of its own: trainers use the modules and dependencies inference uses, without transformers, accelerate, peft, Lightning, Hydra, datasets or webdataset. The host side is `mizuoto/core/training/service.py` (`TrainingService`); the engine side is `mizuoto/training/` (`Trainer`, `StageContext`, `stage.py`) plus each engine's trainer module. Using the Train screen is in Train.","locale":"en"},"14783":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"A training method is `[training.<id>]` in the engine's manifest (`TrainingMethod`). OmniVoice's LoRA:","locale":"en"},"14784":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Field","locale":"en"},"14785":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Meaning","locale":"en"},"14786":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"`stages`","locale":"en"},"14787":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Declared statically, each with `id`, `title`, `help` and `uses` (the hyperparameters it reads; omitted: all). The UI and the fingerprints know the stages without running anything","locale":"en"},"14788":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"`hyperparams`","locale":"en"},"14789":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"`OptionSpec`s; `group = \"basic\"` shows first, `variants` limits one to some variants","locale":"en"},"14790":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"`export`","locale":"en"},"14791":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"`voice_weights` (a voice's own weights, GPT-SoVITS) or `model` (a new user model)","locale":"en"},"14792":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"`variants`","locale":"en"},"14793":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Starts only from models of these variants","locale":"en"},"14794":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"`min_seconds`","locale":"en"},"14795":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"The least audio the dataset must hold","locale":"en"},"14796":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"`assets`","locale":"en"},"14797":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Training-only shared assets per variant (GPT-SoVITS's pretrained discriminators); `\"*\"` for every variant","locale":"en"},"14798":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"`devices`","locale":"en"},"14799":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Device kinds it trains on; the trainer's own `DEVICES` win over it","locale":"en"},"14800":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"`enabled`, `note`","locale":"en"},"14801":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Switch a method off and say why","locale":"en"},"14802":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"The trainer class is the engine's `TRAINER = \"module:Class\"` (see Adding an engine). `TrainingService.methods(model)` says for each method whether it can start from that model and, if not, the `reason`: disabled, wrong variant, the engine runs on a remote host, the model is not installed, the engine is not available, it has no trainer, the device kind does not fit, the trainer's `REQUIRES` do not import, training assets are missing. Each reason carries the command that fixes it.","locale":"en"},"14803":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"The methods today:","locale":"en"},"14804":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Engine","locale":"en"},"14805":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Method","locale":"en"},"14806":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-training#training-methods-in-the-manifest","content":"Stages","locale":"en"},"14807":{"id":"/en/docs/development-training-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-training","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-trainin